897	    // The amounts are kept with two decimals. A blank floor leaves the floor as it is; a blank clerk limit is
898	    // no limit, unless a floor, terms, late percent or policy date are saved with it, when the clerk limit is left as it is (and null
899	    // leaves it as it is too). Anything else that is not an amount of 0.00 or more with up to two decimals is
900	    // refused. Blank terms are left as they are; otherwise they must be a whole number of days, 0 or more.
901	    // A blank late percent is left as it is; otherwise it must be a percent of 0.00 or more, with up to two decimals.
902	    // A missing policy date is left as it is; a blank one is none (the sheet's today is); otherwise it must be a yyyy-MM-dd date.
903	    // A grade's own terms are like the terms: blank is left as it is, otherwise a whole number of days, 0 or more,
904	    // and only a grade (A, B or C) may have them.
905	    // The period numbering from-date is the same: missing is left as it is, blank is off, otherwise a yyyy-MM-dd date.
906	    // The cap is missing: left as it is, blank: none, otherwise an amount of 0.00 or more with up to two decimals.
907	    public void validate(@PathVariable("sheetId") String sheetId, @RequestBody SettingsRequest body,
908	            Out<SheetSettings> settingsOut) throws InvalidSettingException {
909	        String floor = body == null || body.floor() == null ? "" : body.floor().trim();
910	        if (!floor.isEmpty() && !AMOUNT.matcher(floor).matches()) {
911	            throw new InvalidSettingException("The floor must be an amount of 0.00 or more, with up to two decimals.");
912	        }
913	        String typed = body == null || body.clerkLimit() == null ? null : body.clerkLimit().trim();
914	        if (typed != null && !typed.isEmpty() && !AMOUNT.matcher(typed).matches()) {
